ASoC: sunxi: h713-msp: a playback PCM for the box's own sounds, mixed into HDMI in the DSP

The codec's DAC takes one source at a time, and a PC source keeps its HDMI
audio stream open even in silence (PipeWire, 44.1 kHz of nothing), so a
tone on the codec's own PCM was inaudible or refused whenever HDMI played
(doku/102-plan-audio-mischung.md section 1, S16 21:35). Stock never has
that problem: its Android HAL plays system sounds into TridentALSA device 0
(pcm_open(card, 0, ...) in audio.primary.ares.so), the vendor module
snd_alsa_trid.ko puts that device on the audio bridge's input stream 0, a
ring in DRAM that the AUDBRG's RLB channel at 0x06148280 reads and the
AUDIF's I2S transmitter ABPO1 sends into the DSP, and the DSP's path 0x89
"HDMI2PCM_MIXED_TO_SPEAKER" mixes the module DECODER3 - the far end of that
stream - with the HDMI branch in MIXER2 (S23 section 4, S25 section 6).

This adds that ring to the MSP card as one playback PCM, hw:hy310hdmi,0,
48 kHz S16_LE stereo, the format stock's HAL plays, and lets the driver
build stock's mixed graph instead of the plain path 0xC1:

  I2SIN1 -> SURRDEC -> SURRPOSTPRO -> MIXER2.in2/in3  (HDMI)
  DECODER3 -> MIXER2.in0/in1                          (the PCM)
  MIXER2.out0/out1 -> DRC1/DRC2 -> ... -> I2SOUT1      (as before)

with the gain 32 stock's preset gives every mixer input (actions A37..A40)
and, as before, without DELAY1.

The ring is programmed register for register as audbrg_istream_config
@0x32e8 does it - START/END in 16-byte units, the top address nibble in
0x06142044[3:0], CFG 0xAA08 for S16 stereo, STEP one ALSA period so the
channel's STEP interrupt (AUDBRG bit 10+n, SPI 115) is the period
interrupt, a flush pulse and a UMACREQ pulse to start - and the ALSA
pointer is the channel's own read pointer. The transmitter is fed frame by
frame: its DATA register takes a word with the frame length, the channel
in bits 23:22, bit 28 and bit 31, and bit 30 on the first frame
(AudIf_i2so_activate, AudIf_i2so_NewFrameAvail); at the end of every frame
it interrupts (AUDIF bit 18-n, SPI 113) and the handler writes the word
for the next one, exactly what AudIf_i2so_Interrupt does with a full
queue. The clock is 324 MHz / Fs in 8.8 fixed point (AudIf_i2so_setFs).
A ring the channel has stopped reading is reported as an xrun after two
seconds, with the frame count and the AUDIF error word, instead of
leaving aplay waiting for ever. Both interrupt lines are disabled around
every island reset, because an AUDIF access with the audio top clock down
hangs the CPU (S21), and a running stream is stopped when the island goes
down.

A fourth control, "HDMI Input Mute Switch" (DSP 0x8017 bit 0, the mute
stock holds at the I2S input during its own initialisation), silences the
HDMI branch alone, so h713-tv can keep the box's sounds playing while it
quiets HDMI; the master mute keeps its meaning.

Two module parameters exist for the first device test and go away once it
has decided them: istream (0..2, default 0) names the input stream, since
no register we have read pairs an ABPO with DECODER3 - 0 is the one stock
plays system sounds through while HDMI is mixed; graph (0 = path 0xC1 as
today, 1 = path 0x89, 2 = path 0x89 without SURRDEC/SURRPOSTPRO) lets the
surround pair, whose transparency plan 102 lists as open, be taken out
without a rebuild. Without the "audbrg" window and the two interrupts in
the device tree the card is the control-only card it was.

Not written, on purpose: DECODER3's input pre-gain (0x0162 by the index
table of MAPI_AUD_SetInputPrescale), which stock leaves at its reset value
too; it is the first thing to try if the mixed path is silent while the
ring pointer moves. Not decided by a measurement: whether DECODER3 (or the
surround pair) resamples, so a 44.1 kHz HDMI source and the 48 kHz PCM
meet at one rate; the device test of plan 102 section 4 measures it.

